  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 27,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.645,
            0.064,
            0.834,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The Other Side of Zero"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.121,
            0.836,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Surprisingly, negative numbers were still not accepted by many European mathematicians even in the 18th century. Lazare Carnot, a French mathematician in the 18th century, called negative numbers ‘absurd’. But over time, zero as well as negative numbers proved to be indispensable in global mathematics and science, and are now considered to be critical numbers on an equal footing with and as important as positive numbers—just as Brahmagupta had recommended and explicitly described way back in the year 628 CE! This abstraction of arithmetic rules on all numbers paved the way for the modern development of algebra, which we will learn about in future classes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.372,
            0.411,
            0.577,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Summary"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.462,
            0.834,
            0.528
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There are numbers that are less than zero. They are written with a ‘–’ sign in front of them (e.g., – 2), and are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "negative numbers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". They lie to the left of zero on the number line."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.531,
            0.834,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The numbers ..., – 4, – 3, – 2, – 1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, ... are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "integers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, ... are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "positive integers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and the numbers …, – 4, – 3, – 2, – 1 are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "negative integers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Zero (0) is neither positive nor negative."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.622,
            0.834,
            0.71
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Every given number has another number associated to it which when added to the given number gives zero. This is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "additive inverse",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the number. For example, the additive inverse of 7 is – 7 and the additive inverse of – 543 is 543."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.713,
            0.833,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Addition can be interpreted as "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Starting Position + Movement = Target Position",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.758,
            0.833,
            0.801
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Addition can also be interpreted as the combination of movements or increases/decreases: "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Movement 1 + Movement 2 = Total Movement",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.804,
            0.833,
            0.845
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Subtraction can be interpreted as "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Target Position – Starting Position = Movement",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
